\f1\fs22 Alexander threw his first. Chanta saw it coming, not as if it were in slow motion but because it was, by all pugilistic standards, slow. Yet Chanta had no time to block it, especially since his whole body flinched into flinch mode. The punch landed on Chanta\'92s jaw; it stung\'85 a bit, and maybe there\'92d be minor bruising, but it was well short of a knock-out and even failed to threaten Chanta\'92s balance.\
Men who realise they can neither throw nor parry a punch tend to resort to grappling one another, which might rearrange some clothing but otherwise cause neither flesh nor fabric much distress. The grappling becomes a kind of heavy-breathing ballroom tango wrestling which leads inevitably to a foot tripping on one of the other three, and down will come puncher, parrier and all. The fight can end right there if either head-butts the ground, but more commonly leads to the kind of rolling half-wrestle, half-disentangle, half-slap, sometimes half-bite cuddle that is edifying to no observer while the combatants are convinced they are really having at it.\
The Jones clones quickly aimed pistols at Alexander, while the High Elvis permitted the other Elvi to lay bets.\
\'93Only shoot him if I lose,\'94 Chanta grunted to the clones through a grimace, which clarified their objectives not a nanometer.\
They rolled around the ground like two inflatable tube men flattened by a gale. Chanta rolled and must have slammed Alexander\'92s funny bone against the stone floor. Alexander cried out and released his grips. Chanta bounded to his feet; Alexander stood with a little more glue in his gears. Alexander charged Chanta, who stood his ground, hoping to sidestep and throw Alexander. But it was all just weak tea to the kind of high-octane violence they might have considered themselves capable of. Alexander barrelled Chanta in the chest; Chanta twisted with poor timing and the both lost their footing again, hugging each other against the fall.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 They fell into a shallow pond that cushioned very little of their fall.\
They were both beset by fish. Alexander slapped one away. Chanta stood; Alexander leapt at Chanta from a crouch. They toppled again. If either\'92s head caught the lip of the pond they\'92d be dead. Chanta used Alexander\'92s momentum and rolled him against the bronze feature in the middle of the pond. Water poured down the metal onto Alexander\'92s face and threatened to drown him.\
Alexander closed his eyes and mouth against the water but it was still running into his nostrils. He gagged.\
It took him a moment to realise he was no longer being held down. Still on his back, he shimmied out from under the water feature. Chanta was still above him, looking off to the right.\
\'93Sarah,\'94 Chanta said.\
\'93She\'92s mine, tax it,\'94 Alexander almost sobbed. \'93Leave my things alone.\'94\
Chanta looked down at Alexander and pointed in the direction he\'92d been looking. \'93She\'92s over there.\'94
